  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 116 PM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThe upper level low, which is currently over the PNW, will continue \nto bring southerly flow aloft over the rockies today. Due to weaker \nembedded waves making their way over the region, there will rounds \nof showers/thunderstorms today into this evening. Drier conditions \nare expected after 06Z tonight, although an isolated \nshower/thunderstorm or two can't be ruled out.\n\nAs the upper level low approaches the region, there will be an \nenvironment that is conducive to developing stronger storms on \nFriday. However, the threat for severe weather will be dependent on \nwhether there is sufficient shear in the area. A concern associated \nwith a lower shear environment would be the stagnation of the storm \nmovement, which could result in increased risk of excessive rainfall \nand flash flooding. \n\nAs the upper-level low moves eastward and out of the state on \nSaturday/Saturday night, there will be a shift to drier and warmer \nconditions. However, showers may continue to linger on Sunday in \neastern MT. Monday and Tuesday currently look warm and dry. - ESH\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThunderstorms the next few days:\n\nWhile confidence is high that showers and thunderstorms will develop \nover the next few days, confidence in just how much instability \ndevelops remains on the lower side. Relatively strong forcing will \naccompany the passage of this system, particularly on Friday. This \nforcing may ultimately result in a convective initiation that is a \nbit too early to maximize daytime heating, resulting in ample cloud \ncover across much of the region by mid-afternoon that limits \nthunderstorm strength. -AM",
